/*
 * A20OS foreign-architecture guest registry -- X-macro table, read this
 * before adding an entry.
 *
 * This file is not a translation unit.  It is #included by kernel/proc/xlator.c
 * with XLATOR_GUEST defined, and parsed by tools/xlator_fetch.py (guest name ->
 * expected e_machine) and by the check-xlator-guests gate.  Like the syscall
 * tables under kernel/abi/<arch>/, it deliberately contains no #include, no typedef
 * and no declaration of its own.
 *
 *     XLATOR_GUEST(<name>, <e_machine>, <abi>, <argv_template>)
 *
 * Column 1 -- <name>
 *   The cmdline key suffix, so `a20.xlator.<name>=<path>` configures this
 *   guest, and the file name of the cross-built probe (xlate_probe-<name>).
 *   Must be a valid identifier fragment: it reaches both a shell variable
 *   suffix (XLATOR_GUEST_CC_<name> in tools/targets-xlator.mk) and a C string.
 *   It must not contain a dot either -- `.argv` and `.env` are reserved as
 *   the per-guest sub-keys of the configuration surface, so a dot inside a
 *   guest name would make the two ambiguous.
 *
 * Column 2 -- <e_machine>
 *   The ELF e_machine value, spelled as a decimal number so this file stays
 *   free of kernel includes (it cannot say EM_X86_64).  It must agree with the
 *   EM_* constants in kernel/include/mm/elf.h; check-xlator-guests asserts
 *   that, because a typo here would otherwise show up only as a binary that
 *   mysteriously refuses to translate.
 *
 * Column 3 -- <abi>
 *   Which ABI of that machine this row serves: XLATOR_ABI_LINUX or
 *   XLATOR_ABI_NATIVE, spelled as one of those two macros for the same reason
 *   column 2 is a number -- this file cannot include elf.h, and a bare
 *   "linux"/"native" column would be a spelling nothing in the build
 *   cross-checks against the kernel's elf_abi_t.
 *
 *   Why the column exists: both ABIs are ELF64 for the same e_machine, so
 *   the machine alone cannot tell them apart, while the translators that can
 *   run them are disjoint.  qemu-user implements the Linux ABI only, so a
 *   native-ABI image of an architecture that also has a Linux-ABI translator
 *   matches on machine alone and gets forwarded to a program that cannot
 *   load it -- a fault inside the translator instead of an ENOEXEC.  Keying
 *   the lookup on the pair turns that into a configuration miss.
 *
 *   The ABI is derived from the file (elf_probe_foreign() reads
 *   PT_A20_START_INFO), never configured: a row says what this channel *can*
 *   serve, and whether a given image belongs to that row is the loader's
 *   answer.  A native-ABI translator is therefore a new row here, not a new
 *   branch in the loader -- docs/exec-xlator/04-extending.md has the
 *   step-by-step.
 *
 * Column 4 -- <argv_template>
 *   A string literal (quoted, because it becomes a `const char *`): the argv
 *   the translator is invoked with, written as a template over three
 *   substitutions:
 *
 *       @A   the caller's argv[0]
 *       @P   the guest image path
 *       @*   the caller's argv[1..], spliced in place
 *
 *   Everything else becomes a literal argument.  The template must contain
 *   @P, and an unknown @-token is rejected; both rules live in
 *   xlator_parse_template() and are enforced at boot, so a bad template costs
 *   a warning and an unconfigured guest, never a mistranslated exec.
 *
 *   qemu-user needs the explicit @A because guests dispatch on argv[0]:
 *   busybox only enters applet mode when argv[0] contains "busybox", and
 *   otherwise fails with "applet not found".  A translator that takes the
 *   image path as argv[0] itself wants plain "@P @*" instead.
 *
 *   This is a *default*, not a commitment.  The administrator can replace it
 *   per boot with
 *
 *       a20.xlator.<name>.argv=@P,@*
 *
 *   and that is the point of the design: the invocation convention belongs to
 *   the translator binary an administrator points at, not to the guest
 *   architecture, so swapping qemu-user for a differently-shaped translator
 *   is a cmdline change rather than a kernel change.  kernel/include/proc/xlator.h
 *   lists the shapes this covers and, honestly, the one it does not.
 *
 * Adding a guest architecture therefore costs exactly two edits -- one line
 * here, one XLATOR_GUEST_CC_<name> line in tools/targets-xlator.mk -- plus
 * passing `make check-xlator-guests`, which fails if those two disagree in
 * either direction.  Adding a *translator* costs no edit anywhere: it is a
 * path, plus a .argv and/or .env key if its convention differs.
 *
 * A row for an ABI no translator exists for is legal and costs nothing at
 * runtime -- with no path configured, the row is simply never matched, and
 * the boot log says so by name.  That is deliberate: the registry records
 * what the channel can serve, and adding one ahead of the translator is how
 * a gap stays visible instead of turning into a crash later.  The gate only
 * demands that the column names an ABI the kernel knows.
 *
 * NOTE: membership here does not enable anything.  A guest becomes translatable
 * only when the administrator configures a translator path for it on the
 * cmdline; until then it stays on the plain ENOEXEC path.  See
 * docs/exec-xlator/README.md.
 */

XLATOR_GUEST(x86_64,  62,  XLATOR_ABI_LINUX, "-0 @A @P @*")
XLATOR_GUEST(aarch64, 183, XLATOR_ABI_LINUX, "-0 @A @P @*")